hexagon: no device for a DSP that has no correct library

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The host reads the Hexagon version of the chip and loads
libggml-htp-v<N>.so for it. Upstream moves a chip below v73 up to v73, and
takes v73 when the version query fails. Measurements on the v79 silicon of
the OnePlus 13 (2026-09-23) show that a library for a newer DSP than the chip
runs with no fault and gives incorrect values: a v81 library passes ADD,
SOFT_MAX, RMS_NORM, ROPE and SSM_CONV, but every f32 to f16 conversion gives
inf or NaN (MUL_MAT 113 cases, GATED_DELTA_NET 24, SET_ROWS 18, CPY 4),
because the v81-only Q6_Vqf32_equals_Vsf executes on v79 with no trap. Thus
a v68 or v69 chip with the v73 library can give wrong output with no error.

A missing library is the second failure: htp_iface_open fails with
0x80000406, and the exception of the lazy session open ends a program that
does not catch it.

This patch registers no device in three conditions, and the backend then
lists no HTP device, thus an application falls back to its other backends:

- The version query fails. GGML_HEXAGON_ARCH still overrides it.
- The chip is below v73, the oldest version with a library.
- ADSP_LIBRARY_PATH is set, and no directory of it holds the library for the
  chip. An unset path skips this check, because FastRPC then searches its
  own directories.

A chip above v81 still takes the v81 library, with a warning that the
library is not verified on that DSP.
